WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.630
1948.

2
00:00:01.680 --> 00:00:03.240
BBC radio.

3
00:00:03.290 --> 00:00:07.960
The philosopher Bertrand Russell and the
Jesuit Frederick Copleston argue about

4
00:00:08.010 --> 00:00:09.010
God.

5
00:00:09.060 --> 00:00:11.810
Copleston wants a reason
for the whole universe,

6
00:00:11.860 --> 00:00:14.110
one that lies outside it.

7
00:00:14.160 --> 00:00:18.090
Russell's reply is blunt:
the universe is just there,

8
00:00:18.140 --> 00:00:19.450
and that's all.

9
00:00:19.500 --> 00:00:24.040
So does the universe need a cause,
or could it simply be there?

10
00:00:24.090 --> 00:00:29.140
In this mission, you'll test the cosmological
argument against three sharp critics:

11
00:00:29.190 --> 00:00:32.510
David Hume, Russell and Immanuel Kant.

12
00:00:32.560 --> 00:00:35.760
Then you'll see how the Big
Bang is used on both sides,

13
00:00:35.810 --> 00:00:38.410
and map the case for and against.

14
00:00:38.460 --> 00:00:42.610
Your mission: weigh up whether
the universe needs a cause at all.
